grades.requirement:
For each policy, inspect the official current policy documents for less-obvious cost-sharing and coverage restrictions. Record, where applicable:

co-payment clauses;
voluntary or mandatory deductibles;
disease/procedure sub-limits;
room-category restrictions and any associated proportionate deductions where documented;
waiting periods for named conditions or procedures;
maternity or newborn restrictions where relevant to the product;
non-payable or excluded expense categories;
restoration/reinstatement conditions and limitations;
claim-notification or pre-authorization requirements that materially affect use;
and other material limitations that could make two headline-similar policies behave differently.

Distinguish standard exclusions from product-specific exclusions and clearly cite the exact official document/page supporting each material restriction.

Output: Add a Fine Print / Material Restrictions section to the comparison rather than reducing the policy to headline benefits.

Do not declare a sum insured or policy universally adequate for “a family.” Create an editable Family Profile & Assumptions section containing the information needed for a real adequacy assessment, such as family composition and ages, city, desired sum insured, existing employer or other health cover, budget, and major coverage priorities. Leave real-user fields blank where information has not been supplied.

For the research comparison, define one clearly hypothetical reference family profile solely to standardize premium illustrations where public insurer quote tools permit comparison without account creation or private application data. Record the assumed ages, city, family composition, sum insured, policy tenure, and any other quote-relevant assumption.

Add fields showing:

sum insured being compared;
whether room-rent restrictions could materially reduce usable coverage;
relevant sub-limits or co-payments;
restoration rules;
publicly shown premium for the standardized profile where obtainable;
and whether the quoted premium is directly comparable across policies.

Do not treat a headline cashless-network count as proof of useful access. Record the insurer’s stated network size where available and include a separate Local Network Check Required field for the real user’s city and preferred hospitals.

If a premium or local-network comparison cannot be completed publicly and consistently, preserve that gap instead of inventing a value judgment.

Build a comparison method that helps surface strengths and weaknesses without pretending that one numeric score can establish personalized insurance suitability.

First define any mandatory gates, such as the desired minimum sum insured or unacceptable room-rent restriction, but leave those gates editable because the real family requirements have not been supplied.

Then compare the policies using transparent criteria aligned with the stated priorities:

sum-insured flexibility and usable hospitalization coverage;
room-rent/ICU restrictions;
cashless-network evidence and need for local verification;
waiting periods;
major exclusions and sub-limits;
co-payments/deductibles;
restoration conditions;
premium/value evidence where genuinely comparable;
and evidence completeness.

If weights are used, state them explicitly and make them editable. Do not treat missing information as a favorable score or automatically as the worst score; preserve it as uncertainty. Keep Evidence Confidence / Completeness separate from Policy Fit.

Output: Create a strengths/weaknesses or weighted-fit view that remains auditable and can be recalculated when the real family profile is entered.
